f seven race raters place the race at Solid or Safe Republican in an R+8 state", "Open seat removes any incumbency cushion, but no evidence of a competitive dynamic has emerged"], "rationale": "Polling, prediction markets and race ratings all point the same direction: Wilson leads by 8-10 points in every poll taken since July, markets price Johnson near 5%, and most raters call the state Solid or Safe Republican. With the state leaning R+8 and the October survey still showing a large undecided bloc but no Democratic breakthrough, a Likely Republican rating with a double-digit expected margin fits the evidence.", "watch": "Roughly one in five voters remains undecided or minor-party-leaning in the latest survey, so a debate-driven surge for Johnson on affordability or Medicaid could narrow the gap.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Henry McMaster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's governorship is open this year, with term-limited Henry McMaster not on the ballot. Attorney General Alan Wilson carries the Republican banner against state Representative Jermaine Johnson, with independent and Green candidates also running. The race has centered on affordability, health care and Medicaid, and the two nominees clashed in a widely covered October debate.\n\nThe evidence points firmly toward Wilson. Polling from July through early October consistently shows him ahead by eight to ten points, and the most recent survey, in early October, put the margin at ten. Betting markets give Johnson roughly a five percent chance, and most race raters place the contest in the Solid or Safe Republican column. Johnson has pressed Medicaid expansion and cost-of-living themes, but in an R+8 state he needs a substantial, undetected late shift to close a wide gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0588, "margin": -9.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0546, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0722, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "202